# Components and dependency injection

In Upwell 0.20, `#[component]` marks an application value for construction by the container. Register the component in the `app!` builder, then describe its dependencies as fields.

<Example title="Construct a component from a dependency">

```rust plain
use std::sync::Arc;
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[component]
struct Database;
```

Make the consumer a component and use `Arc<T>` for its managed dependency:

```rust plain
#[component]
struct Catalog {
    database: Arc<Database>,
}

impl Catalog {
    async fn find(&self, id: u64) -> String {
        let _ = &self.database;
        format!("catalog item {id}")
    }
}
```

Register both application values in the builder-form host:

```rust plain
app! {
    App::builder("catalog")
        .with_component(Database)
        .with_component(Catalog)
}
```

</Example>

The container owns the constructed component instances. Keep dependencies in the component graph and pass ordinary, per-operation input as method arguments.

## Moving to 1.0

The current [components and injection guide](/docs/1.0.0/di/components) describes the 1.0 component model, including forms and capabilities that are not asserted for 0.20. Apply it while migrating rather than retrofitting its examples to a 0.20 application.
